TITLE:: PltEvents
summary:: Live scatter of events, with a histogram of what has been heard
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltView, Classes/Pbind

DESCRIPTION::
A scrolling window of the last few seconds: one dot per event, log frequency up the
axis, amplitude as radius, over a histogram of the frequencies currently visible.

It shows what a pattern is doing rather than what it was written to do, which is
where the two usually part company.

The view animates at 30 fps and drops events as they scroll out of the window.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: title
argument:: span
Seconds visible.
argument:: freqLo
argument:: freqHi
Range of the frequency axis. The upper panel is logarithmic and labelled in octaves.
argument:: bins
Bins in the histogram.
argument:: width
argument:: height

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: watch
Wrap a pattern so that playing it also plots it, and return the wrapped pattern.

A pattern that sets code::\degree:: or code::\midinote:: has no code::\freq:: of its
own; it is computed through the default parent event, so those spellings plot
correctly too.
argument:: pattern
argument:: freqKey
argument:: ampKey

METHOD:: push
One event. Feed it from a Routine, a responder, or anything else.
argument:: freq
argument:: amp
argument:: time
Defaults to now. Pass it to place an event yourself, which is how an already
recorded sequence gets plotted.

METHOD:: reset
Empty the list.

METHOD:: events
The list itself, as code::[time, freq, amp]:: triples.

METHOD:: span
METHOD:: freqLo
METHOD:: freqHi
METHOD:: bins
METHOD:: maxEvents
Hard cap on the list, in case nothing is scrolling out.
METHOD:: split
Where the panels divide, as a fraction of the height.
METHOD:: dotColor
METHOD:: barColor

EXAMPLES::
code::
(
p = PltEvents("Pwhite", 8, 150, 1200).front;
q = p.watch(
	Pbind(\dur, Pwhite(0.08, 0.3), \freq, Pwhite(200, 900), \amp, Pwhite(0.1, 0.4))
).play;
)
q.stop; p.close;

// the same seen through a scale, which the frequency axis makes obvious
(
p = PltEvents("degrees", 8, 100, 1600).front;
q = p.watch(Pbind(\dur, 0.12, \degree, Pwhite(-7, 14), \amp, 0.2)).play;
)
q.stop; p.close;

// an exponential distribution of frequencies, seen in the histogram below
(
p = PltEvents("Pexprand", 10, 100, 4000, 50).front;
q = p.watch(Pbind(\dur, 0.06, \freq, Pexprand(120, 3500), \amp, 0.15)).play;
)
q.stop; p.close;

// plotting something already recorded
(
p = PltEvents("placed", 8, 150, 1600).front;
~now = Main.elapsedTime;
200.do { |i| p.push(exprand(180.0, 1500.0), rrand(0.05, 0.5), ~now - 8 + (i * 0.04)) };
)
::
